WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
"Iron-Deficiency Anemia: The Best Treatment
and Are Vegetarians at Higher Risk?"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.360
Based on a study
of nearly half a million people

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.240
followed for eight years,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.400
higher consumption
of unprocessed red meat

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.280
and processed meat was
associated with higher risks

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.960
of the #1 killer
of men and women,

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.600
heart disease,
as well as pneumonia,

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.800
diverticular disease—

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.800
outpouchings of the colon wall—

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.960
colon polyps, and diabetes.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.240
Higher poultry intake was
associated with higher risks

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.400
of gastroesophageal
reflux disease—

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.360
acid reflux, gastritis,
and duodenitis,

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.560
which is inflammation of the
stomach lining and intestines,

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.360
as well as diverticular disease
of the colon,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.000
gallbladder disease,
and diabetes.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.080
And all these
were after taking into account

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.000
a vast array of other factors,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.640
including age, sex, class,
education, employment,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.280
smoking, exercise,
even dietary factors,

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.880
like total fruit
and vegetable intake,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.680
and how much fiber they may be
getting from whole grains,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.640
for instance, to help account
for dietary quality.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.520
So, the higher rates of disease
really did seem to be linked

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:02.960
to the amount of meat
they were eating

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.560
instead of other factors.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.960
The researchers
even adjusted for BMI,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.960
meaning it wasn't just
because those eating meat

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.320
tend to be heavier.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.440
The bottom line is that
higher consumption of meat,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.320
including chicken,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:15.840
is associated
with higher risks

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.640
of several
common disease conditions,

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.360
but higher meat consumption
was significantly associated

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.080
with a lower risk
of one condition,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.240
iron deficiency anemia.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.120
In a previous video,
I talked about how vegetarians

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.760
might be right in
the ferritin sweet spot,

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.280
with iron stores low enough

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.920
to help prevent iron
toxicity-related diseases

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.520
like diabetes and cancer,

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.720
but high enough to avoid
iron deficiency anemia.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.880
So, they appear to have
the best of both worlds.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.680
Low iron stores
to keep free radicals down

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.280
but not enough to result
in higher rates of anemia.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.760
At least,
that's what we used to think

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.000
until this study
of British vegetarians

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.440
was published.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.920
Based on the anemia rates
of hundreds of thousands

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.680
of regular red meat eaters,

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.240
those eating red meat
only a few times a week,

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.520
or the thousands of people
who stuck to poultry, to fish,

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.560
were vegetarian
versus hundreds of vegans.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.960
Among white men,
only the fish eaters

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.680
appeared to have significantly
higher rates of anemia.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.760
Among premenopausal women,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.560
the infrequent red meat eaters
had higher rates

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.160
than the regular
red meat eaters,

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.000
and those who skipped
red meat altogether

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.640
had higher rates
except the vegans.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.880
About 9% of regular meat-eating
menstruating women had anemia,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.640
compared to 10% of those who
ate red meat less frequently,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.040
compared to about 13% of those
who didn't eat red meat at all,

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.520
but only 8% of vegans
were anemic.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.760
And postmenopausal women
showed the same trend,

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.920
about 3% higher anemia rates
in those who skipped red meat.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.280
British Indian participants
had higher anemia rates

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.400
across the board,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.240
with significantly higher rates

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.080
in vegetarian men
and older women,

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.040
though not necessarily
among reproductive age women,

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.920
but the rates were
extremely high regardless.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.360
The prevalence among
American women is more like 9%.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.720
Iron deficiency anemia
can have serious consequences,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.320
like this young vegan woman

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.720
who temporarily lost
part of her vision

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.720
due to a severe case of it.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.360
She started out with low iron
stores from her diet,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.520
then experienced menorrhagia,

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.680
meaning excessively
heavy menstrual bleeding,

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.680
which is in fact
the most common cause

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.720
of iron deficiency anemia,
regardless of diet,

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.800
heavy menstrual bleeding.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.360
There's lots of hormonal and
nonhormonal medical treatments

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.880
to treat the cause of anemia
by lightening menstrual flow,

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.200
but in terms
of treating anemia,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.200
oral iron supplements
are considered

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.320
the first line treatment,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.840
though in serious cases

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.520
rapid repletion
of blood levels

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.480
using IV iron can be used.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.480
Intravenous iron
may also be used

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.720
in the setting
of inflammatory bowel disease,

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.080
since oral iron can be toxic
to the lining of the intestines,

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.480
potentially worsening
the disease process.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.640
However, iron supplements
may result

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.840
in adverse gastrointestinal
effects in everybody,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.880
such as abdominal pain,
constipation, or nausea.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.760
Nearly 75% of patients
taking iron supplements

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.160
experience side effects
which ultimately result

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.480
in one in four
ceasing treatment,

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.280
predominantly due
to gastrointestinal problems.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.440
Even if you don't
experience symptoms,

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.080
iron supplements
may exacerbate inflammation

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:21.600
or hurt your microbiome,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.520
reducing the abundance
of beneficial microbes,

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.320
while increasing the abundance
of deleterious ones.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.040
And, of course, iron supplements
are a short-term strategy,

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.440
while dietary interventions
can be considered

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.040
a more long-term strategy.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.280
And indeed,
you can successfully treat

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.920
iron-deficiency anemia
with diet alone,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.480
usually by increasing
consumption of iron-rich foods

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.920
like iron-fortified grains,

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.480
along with vitamin C-rich foods
or beverages

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.560
that help
with iron absorption.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.280
The healthiest iron sources
are legumes

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.960
(beans, split peas,
chickpeas, and lentils),

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.880
whole grains,
dark green leafy vegetables,

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.480
certain fruits,
like dried apricots,

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.160
and nuts and seeds.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.320
Although non-iron
dietary factors,

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.920
such as not drinking tea
with meals

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.760
or adding vitamin C-rich foods,

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.080
don't have as much effect
on iron absorption

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.520
as we used to think,

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.920
it can't hurt
to drink tea separately,

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.160
and include
vitamin C-rich foods,

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:11.840
such as citrus,
tropical fruits,

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.160
bell peppers,
or broccoli with your meals.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.320
If you do need
iron supplements,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.920
the optimal dosing may be
60 to 120 mg of ferrous iron—

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.080
so, like ferrous sulfate,
or ferrous gluconate,

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.920
ferrous lactate,
ferrous fumarate,

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.600
or ferrous succinate—

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.440
though ferrous bisglycinate

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.760
may have at least twice
the bioavailability.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.960
So, you can take half the dose

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.520
and thereby suffer
fewer side effects,

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.360
given with vitamin C
on alternate days.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.680
So, every other day
rather than taken every day.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.400
Oral iron should generally
be taken on an empty stomach,

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.320
at least an hour before meals.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.160
Now, if taking it
on an empty stomach

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.280
causes pain and nausea,

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.600
you can take it with meals
which may reduce side effects

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.600
but may also reduce absorption.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.520
Similarly, taking vitamin C
with the iron

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.680
increases its absorption,
but at the same time,

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.160
increases incidence
of gastrointestinal side effects

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.006
so, only do it
if you can tolerate it.

